messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people are twins or triplets, then they spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings. If people have siblings who are the same age, then they are twins or triplets. If people complain often about annoying siblings, then they have siblings who are the same age. If people do not live at home regularly, then they do not develop strong bonds with their family members. If people spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings, then they often did not live at home regularly. Luke is either both a twin or triplet and develops strong bonds with his family members, or he neither is a twin or triplet nor develops strong bonds with his family members.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Luke spends a lot of time hanging out and playing with his siblings.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Luke spends a lot of time hanging out and playing with his siblings" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s list out all the given premises:\n\n1.If people are twins or triplets, then they spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings.\n2.If people have siblings who are the same age, then they are twins or triplets.\n3.If people complain often about annoying siblings, then they have siblings who are the same age.\n4.If people do not live at home regularly, then they do not develop strong bonds with their family members.\n5.If people spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings, then they often did not live at home regularly.\n6.Luke is either both a twin or triplet and develops strong bonds with his family members, or he neither is a twin or triplet nor develops strong bonds with his family members.\n\nNow, the question is about whether Luke spends a lot of time with his siblings.Let\'s see how the premises connect.\n\nFrom premise 6, Luke has two possibilities:\n- He is a twin/triplet and has strong bonds.\n- He is neither a twin/triplet nor has strong bonds.\n\nLet\'s consider each possibility.\n\nFirst possibility: Luke is a twin/triplet and has strong bonds.\n\nIf Luke is a twin/triplet, from premise 1, he spends a lot of time with his siblings.So in this case, the statement is true.\n\nSecond possibility: Luke is neither a twin/triplet nor has strong bonds.\n\nIf he\'s not a twin/triplet, does that mean he doesn\'t spend time with his siblings?Let\'s see.\n\nFrom premise 2, having siblings of the same age implies being twins/triplets.So if Luke isn\'t a twin/triplet, he doesn\'t have siblings of the same age.Therefore, he doesn\'t have siblings who are the same age.\n\nIf he doesn\'t have siblings of the same age, premise 3 says that if someone complains about annoying siblings, they have same-age siblings.But since Luke doesn\'t have same-age siblings, he doesn\'t complain about them.However, this doesn\'t directly tell us about his time spent with siblings.\n\nLooking at premise 4: If someone doesn\'t live at home regularly, they don\'t develop strong bonds.The contrapositive is that if someone does develop strong bonds, they live at home regularly.\n\nFrom premise 5: If someone spends a lot of time with siblings, then they often didn\'t live at home regularly.So spending time with siblings implies not living at home regularly, which would mean they don\'t develop strong bonds (from premise 4).\n\nBut in the second possibility, Luke does not develop strong bonds.So according to premise 5, if he spends time with siblings, he doesn\'t live at home, which would mean he doesn\'t develop strong bonds.But in this case, he doesn\'t develop strong bonds, so it\'s possible that he spends time with siblings, but that would mean he doesn\'t live at home.However, premise 4 says that not living at home leads to not developing strong bonds, which aligns.\n\nWait, but if Luke doesn\'t develop strong bonds, does that mean he doesn\'t spend time with siblings?Because premise 5 says that spending time with siblings implies not living at home, which implies not developing strong bonds.So if he doesn\'t develop strong bonds, it\'s possible that he spends time with siblings, but it\'s also possible that he doesn\'t.\n\nBut in the second possibility, Luke is neither a twin/triplet nor has strong bonds.So he doesn\'t have same-age siblings, but he might have older or younger siblings.The premises don\'t specify about non-same-age siblings.So he could have siblings of different ages, but the premises don\'t say anything about whether he spends time with them or not.\n\nWait, premise 1 only applies if he\'s a twin/triplet, which he isn\'t in this case.So premise 1 doesn\'t apply.Premise 2 is about same-age siblings implying twins/triplets, which he doesn\'t have.So he doesn\'t have same-age siblings, but he might have siblings of different ages.\n\nBut the question is about whether he spends a lot of time with his siblings.The premises don\'t directly address this unless he\'s a twin/triplet or has same-age siblings.Since he isn\'t, we don\'t have information about his time spent with siblings.\nIf Luke is a twin/triplet, he doesn\'t spend a lot of time with his siblings (so statement is false).\nIf he\'s not a twin/triplet, he does have same-age siblings, and the premises do say he spends time with siblings of different ages. So in this case, we do know.\nTherefore, the statement could be false or certain. But wait, the options are true, false, or uncertain. So since in one scenario it\'s false and in the other it\'s certain, the overall answer would be certain.\nWait, but let me think again. The question is whether the statement is true, false, or uncertain. So based on the premises, we can definitively say that Luke spends a lot of time with his siblings because it depends on whether he\'s a twin/triplet or not. Since premise 6 says he\'s either both a twin/triplet and has strong bonds, or neither. So if he\'s a twin/triplet, he doesn\'t spend time. If he\'s not, we do know because the premises do cover that scenario.\nTherefore, the statement is certain because we have enough information to confirm it in the case where he\'s neither a twin/triplet nor has strong bonds.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement is uncertain because while Luke could spend time with siblings if he\'s a twin/triplet, the premises don\'t provide information about his sibling interactions if he isn\'t. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 23%|##2       | 46/204 [49:07<1:47:21, 40.77s/it]
