messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nSusan flies to LGA airport. The departure and arrival can not be the same airport. John flies from LGA airport.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? John flies to LGA airport.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "John flies to LGA airport" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that Susan flies to LGA airport.So, Susan\'s destination is LGA.It also mentions that the departure and arrival can\'t be the same airport for her.That means Susan must be flying from somewhere else to LGA, not staying at the same airport.\n\nThen, it says John flies from LGA airport.So, John\'s departure point is LGA.Now, the question is about whether John flies to LGA airport.Wait, that doesn\'t make sense because if John is flying from LGA, he can\'t be flying to the same airport.The context already states that for Susan, departure and arrival can\'t be the same, but does that rule apply to John as well?\n\nHmm, the context only specifies that Susan\'s departure and arrival can\'t be the same.It doesn\'t explicitly say that this rule applies to John.So, maybe John could be flying to LGA, but that would mean he\'s flying from somewhere else to LGA.But wait, the context says John flies from LGA, so his departure is LGA.If he\'s flying to LGA, that would mean he\'s flying from LGA to LGA, which is the same airport.But the context only mentions Susan\'s case, not John\'s.So, does the rule apply to John or not?\n\nWait, the context says "Susan flies to LGA airport.The departure and arrival can not be the same airport."So, that rule is specifically about Susan.It doesn\'t say anything about John.So, for Susan, she can\'t depart and 