 Bonnie is a student (C is true), she could be A (young and wishes to further) or not A (maybe older student, but that\'s not specified).\n\nBut let\'s proceed.\n\nCase 1: C is true (Bonnie is a student).\n\nFrom premise 2: P ∨ D.\n\nIf C is true, then from premise 1, if P is true, then C is true, which is consistent.\n\nIf D is true, then from premise 4, B is true, and from premise 3, B → ¬C, which contradicts C being true.So, in this case, D must be false, so P must be true.\n\nSo, in Case 1, C is true, so D is false, and P is true.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the statement: (A ∧ B) ∨ (¬A ∧ ¬B) → (C ∨ D).\n\nSince C is true, the consequent (C ∨ D) is true regardless of D.\n\nSo, the implication is true in this case.\n\nCase 2: C is false (Bonnie is not a student).\n\nFrom premise 2: P ∨ D.\n\nIf C is false, then from premise 1, P → C, which would mean P is false (because if P were true, C would have to be true, which it\'s not).So, P is false.\n\nTherefore, from premise 2, since P is false, D must be true.\n\nSo, in this case, D is true.\n\nFrom premise 4: D → B, so B is true.\n\nFrom premise 3: B → ¬C, which is consistent because C is false.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the statement: (A ∧ B) ∨ (¬A ∧ ¬B) → (C ∨ D).\n\nSince C is false and D is true, the consequent (C ∨ D) is true.\n\nSo, the implication is true in this case as well.\n\nWait, but let me check the antecedent in each case.\n\nIn Case 1: C is true.\n\nWhat is the antecedent (A ∧ B) ∨ (¬A ∧ ¬B)?\n\nWe know that in Case 1, C is true, so from premise 5, if A is true, then C is true, which it is.So, A could be true or false.\n\nBut let\'s see:\n\nIf A is true, then from premise 5, C is true, which it is.\n\nFrom premise 2, since C is true, P is true (as D is false).\n\nFrom premise 1, P → C, which is true.\n\nNow, what about B?\n\nFrom premise 4: D → B.Since D is false, B could be either true or false.\n\nWait, no, in Case 1, C is true, so from premise 3, if B were true, then C would have to be false, which it\'s not.So, B must be false in this case.\n\nWait, that\'s a contradiction.Let me think again.\n\nWait, in Case 1, C is true.\n\nFrom premise 2: P ∨ D.\n\nSince C is true, and from premise 1, P → C, which is true, so P could be true or false.\n\nBut if P is true, then from premise 1, C is true, which is consistent.\n\nIf P is false, then from premise 2, D must be true, but D → B, and B → ¬C, which would make C false, contradicting our assumption that C is true.Therefore, in Case 1, P must be true, and D must be false.\n\nSo, in Case 1, P is true, D is false, and from premise 4, D is false, so B could be either true or false?Wait, no, because premise 4 says D → B, but if D is false, B can be anything.However, from premise 3, B → ¬C.Since C is true, B must be false (because if B were true, then ¬C would have to be true, which it\'s not).So, in Case 1, B must be false.\n\nSo, in Case 1:\n\nA can be true or false.\n\nB is false.\n\nSo, the antecedent is (A ∧ B) ∨ (¬A ∧ ¬B).\n\nSince B is false, (A ∧ B) is false.\n\nSo, the antecedent becomes (¬A ∧ ¬B).\n\nBut ¬B is true, so it\'s (¬A ∧ true) → ¬A.\n\nSo, the antecedent is ¬A.\n\nNow, the consequent is (C ∨ D) → (true ∨ false) → true.\n\nSo, the implication is: if ¬A, then true.Which is always true, because the consequent is true.\n\nAlternatively, if A is true, then the antecedent is (A ∧ B) ∨ (¬A ∧ ¬B) → (true ∧ false) ∨ (false ∧ true) → false ∨ false → false.So, the implication is false → true, which is true.\n\nWait, no, if A is true, then the antecedent is (A ∧ B) ∨ (¬A ∧ ¬B) → (true ∧ false) ∨ (false ∧ true) → false ∨ false → false.So, the implication is false → true, which is true.\n\nIf A is false, then the antecedent is (false ∧ false) ∨ (true ∧ true) → false ∨ true → true.So, the implication is true → true, which is true.\n\nSo, in Case 1, regardless of A, the implication is true.\n\nIn Case 2: C is false.\n\nFrom premise 2, since C is false, P must be false (because if P were true, C would have to be true, which it