nes (B), and thus cannot be a student (\u00acC).\n\n3. **Case Analysis:**\n   - **Case 1: Bonnie is a student (C).**\n     - From premise 5, A \u2192 C.\n     - From premise 2, P \u2228 D. Since C is true, P must be true (as D would imply \u00acC).\n     - From premise 1, P \u2192 C, consistent.\n     - B must be false (as B \u2192 \u00acC).\n     - The antecedent (A \u2227 B) \u2228 (\u00acA \u2227 \u00acB) is false, making the implication true.\n   - **Case 2: Bonnie is not a student (\u00acC).**\n     - From premise 2, P \u2228 D. Since \u00acC, P must be false, so D must be true.\n     - From premise 4, D \u2192 B.\n     - From premise 3, B \u2192 \u00acC, consistent.\n     - The antecedent (A \u2227 B) \u2228 (\u00acA \u2227 \u00acB) is false, making the implication true.\n   - **Case 3: Bonnie is neither A nor B.**\n     - From premise 2, P must be true (as D is false).\n     - From premise 1, P \u2192 C, so C is true.\n     - The antecedent (A \u2227 B) \u2228 (\u00acA \u2227 \u00acB) is true, and the consequent (C \u2228 D) is true, making the implication true.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - In all cases, the implication holds true.\n\nThus, the statement is true.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
