      "text": " B) False.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding Premises:**\n   - Premise 1: Spilling food \u2192 Not tidy and enjoy cleaning.\n   - Premise 2: Foodie (goes out frequently) \u2192 Spills food.\n   - Premise 3: Only child \u2192 Tidy and enjoy cleaning.\n   - Premise 4: Busy parents \u2192 Only child.\n   - Premise 5: High net income \u2192 Busy parents.\n   - Premise 6: Peter is either (Spills \u2227 Only child) or (\u00acSpills \u2227 \u00acOnly child).\n\n2. **Analyzing Peter's Status:**\n   - If Peter is both a spiller and an only child, it leads to a contradiction because spillers don't tidy, but only children do. Thus, Peter cannot be both.\n   - Therefore, Peter must be neither a spiller nor an only child.\n\n3. **Implications for Being a Foodie:**\n   - Since Peter doesn't spill food (from Premise 6), he cannot be a foodie who goes out frequently (contrapositive of Premise 2).\n\n4. **Implications for Household Income:**\n   - If Peter's household had a high net income, Premise 5 implies his parents are busy, leading to Premise 4 (only child). But Peter isn't an only child, so his household doesn't have a high net income.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - Peter is neither a foodie nor in a high-income household, making the statement false.\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
