messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAll employees who schedule a meeting with their customers will appear in the company today. Everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings with their customers. Employees will either have lunch in the company or have lunch at home. If an employee has lunch at home, then he/she is working remotely from home. All employees who are in other countries work remotely from home. No managers work remotely from home. James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? James has lunch in the company.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "James has lunch in the company" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given information to make it clearer:\n\n1.All employees who schedule a meeting with their customers will appear in the company today.\n2.Everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings with their customers.\n3.Employees will either have lunch in the company or have lunch at home.\n4.If an employee has lunch at home, then he/she is working remotely from home.\n5.All employees who are in other countries work remotely from home.\n6.No managers work remotely from home.\n7.James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\n\nNow, the question is about whether James has lunch in the company.Let\'s see how each of these points can help us determine that.\n\nStarting with point 3: Employees have lunch either at the company or at home.So, James must be doing one or the other.If we can figure out which one, we can answer the question.\n\nPoint 4 says that if someone has lunch at home, they\'re working remotely.Point 5 adds that employees in other countries work remotely.So, if James is in another country, he\'s working remotely, which would mean he\'s having lunch at home.But we don\'t know if James is in another country or not.So that\'s a possibility, but not certain.\n\nPoint 6 states that no managers work remotely.So, if James is a manager, he can\'t be working remotely, which means he can\'t be having lunch at home.Therefore, if James is a manager, he must be having lunch at the company.\n\nNow, looking at point 7: James is either a manager and appears in the company today, or he\'s neither a manager nor appears in the company today.So, there are two possibilities for James:\n\na) He is a manager and appears in the company today.\nb) He is not a manager and does not appear in the company today.\n\nLet\'s consider each scenario.\n\nCase a: James is a manager and appears in the company today.\n\nFrom point 6, since he\'s a manager, he doesn\'t work remotely.So, he can\'t be having lunch at home.Therefore, he must be having lunch at the company.Additionally, from point 2, everyone who has lunch at the company schedules meetings.And from point 1, those who schedule meetings appear in the company.So, this all fits together.In this case, James has lunch at the company.\n\nCase b: James is neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\n\nIf he doesn\'t appear in the company, let\'s see what that implies.From point 1, if someone schedules a meeting, they appear in the company.So, if James doesn\'t appear, he didn\'t schedule a meeting.From point 2, if someone has lunch at the company, they schedule a meeting.Therefore, if James didn\'t schedule a meeting, he didn\'t have lunch at the company.So, he must have had lunch at home.\n\nBut wait, if he had lunch at home, from point 4, he\'s working remotely.From point 5, if he\'s in another country, he\'s working remotely.But we don\'t know if he\'s in another country.James is definitely not having lunch at home, as he is a manager who works at the company. The statement "James has lunch in the company" is undoubtedly true, given his role and presence at work.</think>.\n\nThe statement "James has lunch in the company" is uncertain because James could either be a manager (and thus have lunch at the company) or not a manager (and thus have lunch at home). Without additional information, we can\'t determine which scenario applies. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
