messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAll mammals are animals. All monkeys are mammals. An animal is either a monkey or a bird. All birds fly. All animals breathe. If something can fly, then it has wings. If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Rock does not have wings.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve.Let me try to break it down step by step.The context gives several statements, and I need to figure out whether the statement "Rock does not have wings" is true, false, or uncertain based on that context.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given premises to make sure I don\'t miss anything:\n\n1.All mammals are animals.\n2.All monkeys are mammals.\n3.An animal is either a monkey or a bird.\n4.All birds fly.\n5.All animals breathe.\n6.If something can fly, then it has wings.\n7.If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.\n\nThe question is about whether Rock does not have wings.So I need to determine the truth value of that statement based on the premises.\n\nLet me start by analyzing each premise and see how they connect.\n\nPremise 1: All mammals are animals.So, mammals are a subset of animals.\n\nPremise 2: All monkeys are mammals.So, monkeys are a subset of mammals, which in turn are a subset of animals.\n\nPremise 3: An animal is either a monkey or a bird.So, every animal is either a monkey or a bird, but not both?Or can they be both?The wording says "either a monkey or a bird," which typically implies exclusive or, meaning an animal is one or the other, not both.So, the set of animals is divided into monkeys and birds, with no overlap.\n\nPremise 4: All birds fly.So, every bird has the ability to fly.\n\nPremise 5: All animals breathe.So, every animal, whether monkey or bird, can breathe.\n\nPremise 6: If something can fly, then it has wings.So, flying implies having wings.But does having wings imply flying?Not necessarily.It just says that if you can fly, you must have wings.\n\nPremise 7: If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.Wait, that\'s a bit confusing.Let me parse it.So, if Rock is not a fly and not a bird, then Rock doesn\'t fly and doesn\'t breathe.But wait, premise 5 says all animals breathe.So, if Rock is an animal, it must breathe.But premise 7 says that if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it doesn\'t breathe.That seems contradictory unless Rock is not an animal.\n\nWait, let me think again.Premise 7 is a conditional statement: If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.So, the antecedent is "Rock is neither a fly nor a bird," and the consequent is "Rock neither flies nor breathes."\n\nBut from premise 5, all animals breathe.So, if Rock is an animal, it must breathe.Therefore, if Rock is an animal, the consequent of premise 7 ("Rock neither flies nor breathes") would be false because Rock does breathe.Therefore, the antecedent must also be false for the implication to hold.So, if Rock is an animal, then the antecedent "Rock is neither a fly nor a bird" must be false.That means Rock must be either a fly or a bird.\n\nWait, but premise 3 says that animals are either monkeys or birds.So, if Rock is an animal, it\'s either a monkey or a bird.But premise 7 says if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it doesn\'t fly or breathe.But if Rock is a bird, then from premise 4, it flies.So, if Rock is a bird, it flies, which would mean it has wings (from premise 6).If Rock is a monkey, then it\'s a mammal (from premise 2), and since all mammals are animals (premise 1), it\'s an animal.Monkeys don\'t fly, so they don\'t have wings?Wait, no, premise 6 says if something can fly, then it has wings.It doesn\'t say that only flying things have wings.So, maybe some non-flying things could have wings, but according to premise 6, it\'s only that flying implies wings, not the other way around.\n\nWait, but let\'s go back.Premise 7: If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.But if Rock is an animal, it must breathe (premise 5).So, if Rock is an animal, then the consequent of premise 7 ("Rock neither flies nor breathes") is false because Rock does breathe.Therefore, the antecedent must be false, meaning Rock is either a fly or a bird.\n\nBut wait, premise 3 says animals are either monkeys or birds.So, if Rock is an animal, it\'s either a monkey or a bird.So, if Rock is a bird, then from premise 4, it flies, and from premise 6, it has wings.If Rock is a monkey, then it\'s a mammal, and since all mammals are animals, it\'s an animal.Monkeys don\'t fly, so they don\'t have wings?Or do they?Wait, premise 6 only says that if something flies, it has wings.It doesn\'t say that only flying things have wings.So, maybe some non-flying animals have wings, but that\'s not stated.So, if Rock is a monkey, it\'s a mammal, an animal, but it doesn\'t fly, so premise 6 doesn\'t say anything about whether it has wings or not.So, Rock could have wings or not, but since it doesn\'t fly, premise 6 doesn\'t require it to have wings.\n\nBut wait, let\'s think about premise 7 again.If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it neither flies nor breathes.But if Rock is an animal, it must breathe (premise 5).So, if Rock is an animal, it must breathe, which would make the consequent of premise 7 ("Rock neither flies nor breathes") false.Therefore, the antecedent must be false, meaning Rock is either a fly or a bird.\n\nBut wait, premise 3 says animals are either monkeys or birds.So, if Rock is an animal, it\'s either a monkey or a bird.So, if Rock is a bird, it flies (premise 4) and thus has wings (premise 6).If Rock is a monkey, it\'s a mammal, an animal, but it doesn\'t fly, so premise 6 doesn\'t apply.So, Rock could have wings or not, but since it\'s a monkey, which typically don\'t have wings, but the premises don\'t specify that.So, the only thing we can say for sure is that if Rock is a bird, it has wings.If Rock is a monkey, we don\'t know.\n\nBut wait, premise 7 says if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it neither flies nor breathes.But if Rock is an animal, it must breathe, so the consequent is false, meaning the antecedent is false.Therefore, Rock must be either a fly or a bird.But premise 3 says animals are either monkeys or birds.So, if Rock is an animal, it\'s either a monkey or a bird.But premise 7 says Rock must be either a fly or a bird.So, if Rock is a monkey, it\'s an animal, but premise 7 says Rock must be a fly or a bird.Wait, that\'s a contradiction because monkeys are animals but not flies or birds.So, perhaps Rock is not an animal.\n\nWait, that\'s a possibility.Let me think.If Rock is not an animal, then premise 5 doesn\'t apply, so Rock might not breathe.But premise 7 says if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it neither flies nor breathes.So, if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it doesn\'t fly and doesn\'t breathe.But if Rock is an animal, it must breathe, so Rock can\'t be neither a fly nor a bird.Therefore, Rock must be either a fly or a bird.\n\nBut wait, premise 3 says animals are either monkeys or birds.So, if Rock is an animal, it\'s either a monkey or a bird.But premise 7 says Rock must be a fly or a bird.So, if Rock is a monkey, it\'s an animal, but premise 7 says Rock must be a fly or a bird.That seems conflicting.Therefore, perhaps Rock is not an animal.\n\nWait, let me try to structure this.\n\nFrom premise 7: If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.\n\nBut from premise 5: All animals breathe.\n\nSo, if Rock is an animal, it must breathe.Therefore, the consequent of premise 7 ("Rock neither flies nor breathes") would be false because Rock does