 leading to D being true. Therefore, the implication holds, making the statement true. So, the correct answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that if L and A are false, D is false, but that doesn't make the implication false because the implication is only false when the antecedent is true and the consequent is false. So, the statement is true because whenever L and A are true, D is true, and when they're false, the implication is true regardless of D. Therefore, the correct answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but I'm still a bit confused. Let me try to outline it step by step.\n\n1. If L, then W. So, L \u2192 W.\n2. If A, then C. So, A \u2192 C.\n3. If (C \u2227 W), then D. So, (C \u2227 W) \u2192 D.\n\nWe need to see if (L \u2227 A) \u2192 D is true.\n\nAssume L \u2227 A is true. Then, L is true and A is true.\n\nFrom L \u2192 W, since L is true, W must be true.\n\nFrom A \u2192 C, since A is true, C must be true.\n\nNow, we have both W and C true. From (C \u2227 W) \u2192 D, since both are true, D must be true.\n\nTherefore, whenever L \u2227 A is true, D is true. Hence, (L \u2227 A) \u2192 D is true.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true. So, the correct answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that if L and A are false, D is false, but that doesn't affect the implication's truth value because the implication is only false when the antecedent is true and the consequent is false. So, in all cases, the implication holds. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nYes, I think that's correct. So, the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but I'm still a bit unsure because sometimes in logic, there might be hidden assumptions. Let me think again.\n\nIf George likes music and has access to a program, does he compose?\n\nFrom the premises:\n\n- L \u2192 W: So, if he likes music, he wants to compose.\n- A \u2192 C: If he has access, he can compose.\n- (C \u2227 W) \u2192 D: If he can and wants to compose, he will compose.\n\nSo, if L and A are both true, then W and C are bot