 Y \\) and \\( I \\).\n   - In logical terms: \\( (C \\lor P) \\rightarrow (Y \\land I) \\).\n\n4. **Case Analysis**:\n   - **Case 1**: Bonnie chaperones (\\( C \\) is true).\n     - From Premise 3: \\( C \\rightarrow \\neg S \\). Bonnie is not a student.\n     - From Premise 6: Bonnie is either \\( (A \\land S) \\) or \\( (\\neg A \\land \\neg S) \\). Since \\( \\neg S \\), she is \\( (\\neg A \\land \\neg S) \\).\n     - From Premise 5: \\( Y \\rightarrow S \\). Since \\( \\neg S \\), \\( Y \\) is false.\n     - From Premise 2: \\( P \\lor I \\). Since \\( \\neg P \\), \\( I \\) is true.\n     - Consequent: \\( Y \\land I \\) is false (since \\( Y \\) is false).\n   - **Case 2**: Bonnie does not chaperone (\\( \\neg C \\)) but performs often (\\( P \\) is true).\n     - From Premise 1: \\( P \\rightarrow A \\). Bonnie attends and is engaged.\n     - From Premise 6: Bonnie is \\( (A \\land S) \\).\n     - From Premise 5: \\( Y \\rightarrow S \\). Bonnie could be \\( Y \\) or not.\n     - From Premise 4: \\( I \\rightarrow C \\). Since \\( \\neg C \\), \\( I \\) is false.\n     - Consequent: \\( Y \\land I \\) is false (since \\( I \\) is false).\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - In both cases, the antecedent is true, but the consequent is false. Therefore, the implication is false.\n\n### Final Answer:\n\\boxed{B}",
